Dear 3D ADEPT Editorial Team,

Solidon3D 0.3.3 offers a software story at the intersection of AM workflow,
data control and AI governance: the language model is an optional interface to
a fixed operation API, not a geometry engine, and the current version extends
that API to feature-level editing. Moving a recognised feature is what was
missing there for a long time.

Every model change is a registered, parameterised operation evaluated by
deterministic geometry code. The same operation can be requested from a menu,
shortcut or chat. The model cannot execute source code, and executable code is
never embedded in a project file.

Applied to practical preparation tasks, 0.3.3 adds:

* recognised holes, pegs, countersinks and domes in imported STL or 3MF can be
  moved, rotated, resized, duplicated and removed, with the original axis and
  the feature identity preserved, so dependent fits keep their reference;
* the measured values of a selected feature are presented as editable fields
  with a live preview, and one option applies an edit to every feature of the
  same kind as a single transaction;
* recognition itself is bounded by manufacturability: features below half a
  millimetre are discarded, which removed 296 of 1130 entries on a sample part;
* where an action is geometrically impossible for a feature type, the software
  states the reason in a sentence instead of greying out a control.

All modelling, analysis and export remain available without AI, an account or
an internet connection. Solidon runs on Windows, macOS and Linux and keeps
project files local.

Product: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Changelog: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

I can provide the released 0.3.3 build, an architecture note, a safe project
file example and the resulting STL-to-3MF workflow for editorial review.

